}', 'Work through the example line by line. It demonstrates useState and useEffect, and the rules of hooks. The first lines set up the context; the middle lines do the main work described by the two key ideas above; the final lines produce the result you can check in a browser, editor or terminal. Change one value, re-run it, and confirm that the change behaves as you predicted — that check is the real learning.', 'Build a small, real example of state and hooks: useState and useEffect, and the rules of hooks. Use the code example as a starting point, change it to fit your own example, and check that it works. Write down one thing that surprised you and one thing you would do differently next time. You may not mark this topic complete until you have finished the lesson and passed its quiz (7/10).', '[{"mistake":"Skipping the idea and copying the code example without understanding it.","correction":"Read each line, change one thing, and observe what changes. Understanding survives; copying does not."},{"mistake":"Treating state and hooks as a fact to memorise instead of a skill to practise.","correction":"Practise on a small real example the same day. A topic is only complete after its lesson and a passed quiz (7/10)."},{"mistake":"Ignoring error messages and warnings while working on state and hooks.","correction":"Read the message, note the file and line, and fix the named problem before moving on."}]'::jsonb, 'In short: State and Hooks is about useState and useEffect, and the rules of hooks. Remember that state is data a component owns and re-renders when it changes, and that hooks must be called at the top level of a component, never inside conditions. Practise it on a real example, check your work, and then take the 10-question quiz — 7 correct answers passes the topic.', 1 from public.topics where slug = 'state-and-hooks' on conflict (topic_id, version) do nothing;
